module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.container.{Map, map}
import sysl.posix.tty.{flush}
import sysl.text.{from_utf8_unchecked, str_builder, Search, StrBuilder}

// The functions a slate program has without writing them.
//
// **Each is an ordinary value bound in the root scope**, so `val p = print` binds it and
// `apply(print, 1)` calls it. A builtin dispatched on the name at the call site would have been the
// one kind of function a program could not pass around, which in a language where functions are
// values is a hole rather than a simplification.
//
// **The LIST of them is in `natives.sysl` and how a value is PRINTED is in `render.sysl`.** What is
// here is the dispatch and the implementations: `call_native` and the `do_*` functions it reaches.
// Nothing private crosses either cut in either direction, which is what made both of them the cuts
// to make.
//
// **The four a test is written with are `assert.sysl`**, cut out on the section heading that was
// already here when this file passed a thousand lines. Those four cross -- `dispatch_native` calls
// them by name -- and they are the four that answer whether a test passed rather than answering a
// value.
// Everything the program printed, in order, is `current().output`.
//
// **A field of the VM (`vm_state.sysl`), which is what a second interpreter in one process needs.**
// It was module storage until then, because a root function has to be a top-level function to have
// an address and has to reach the interpreter's state -- so that state could not be a local of
// anything. `run` clears it, which is what lets two programs run one after the other.

// Whether `print` also reaches the real descriptor as each call happens, rather than only building
// up `output` for whoever asked to run this program to replay once the whole thing has finished.
//
// **Off by default, because most callers of `run` are asking a question rather than running a
// program.** A test that executes a snippet of slate to check what it printed wants the answer as a
// value, not scattered across the gate's own terminal thousands of times over -- and the doc-test
// harness compares `output` to a fenced block, which a stream already consumed cannot be compared
// against. `run_program` in `main.sysl` turns this on because it is not asking a question: a program
// run from the command line may never return, and a supervisor's first line has to reach a reader
// before its hundredth line does. It is `current().live_output`.

// Where a live `print` goes: `main.sysl`'s `say`, which is `run_cli`'s caller's choice of where the
// driver's own words go. A test drives the CLI in-process with a sink of its own rather than the
// real descriptor (`cli_said` in `tests_kit_cli.sysl`), and a program run through it is reached by
// `run_program` exactly as a real invocation is -- so what `live_output` turns on has to call
// WHATEVER `say` this run was given, not the real `print`, or a captured run would say nothing and a
// captured run's sink would never see what its own program printed.
//
// Not `private`: `run_program` in `main.sysl` puts `live_sink` back to this once the run it started
// is over.
no_live_sink(line: string) = ()

// The sink itself is `current().live_sink`.

// Whether the test now running asked to be left out, and the reason it gave, are
// `current().skip_asked` and `current().skip_reason`.
//
// **Read by the runner rather than matched on the signal, and the asynchronous case is why.** A
// `skip` inside an `async` test travels out of the coroutine and settles its promise as a rejection,
// which keeps the sentence and loses which variant it was -- so the fact has to be written down where
// `skip` was called. `run_one_test` clears both before every test.

// **EVERY ARGUMENT IS ROOTED ACROSS THE CALL, AND THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THAT SAYS SO.** `args` is a
// sysl `Buf`, which the collector does not walk -- and a call has already popped the callee and its
// arguments off the operand stack by the time it gets here, so for the length of a native call the
// only thing holding them is this buffer. That is invisible for every builtin that answers without
// running slate code, and fatal for the ten or so that call back into it: `map` walks a receiver and
// calls a function, and a callback that allocates hard enough to collect frees BOTH of them.
//
// **What it looks like is never a memory failure.** `["a", "b", "c"].map(r -> burn(500))` failed with
// *"this array was changed while `map` was walking it"* about a literal nothing can reach; binding the
// array to a `val` moved the failure onto the callback -- *"this function takes from 1 to 0
// arguments"* -- because the freed slot had been taken by something else and read back as a function
// of a different arity. Binding both made it pass, and the JavaScript back end passed throughout.
//
// **Rooting here rather than in each `do_*` is the point.** The hole was in `map`, and it was in
// `filter`, `reduce`, `sort`, `find` and every other builtin that calls back -- one rule at the
// dispatch cannot be half-applied, where a rule written out per builtin already had been.
call_native(f: NativeFn, args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    val mark = root_mark()

    for i in 0..<args.len()
        hold(args.at(i))

    // **Two clock readings, or none at all where the profiler is off** -- `profile_began` answers
    // zero without reading a clock, and `profile_native` returns on the same flag. A builtin that
    // runs a slate callback is timed inclusively; `profile.sysl` says so.
    val began = profile_began()
    val answer = dispatch_native(f, args, at)

    profile_native(f, began)

    // Nothing allocates between here and the caller pushing the answer, which is the same window
    // every `do_*` already ends in.
    release(mark)
    answer
end call_native

// **One indirection and no match.** A builtin's id is its slot in `natives`, so what used to be a
// 371-line match over every arm of a closed enum is a bounds check and a call through the entry the
// registry already holds.
private dispatch_native(f: NativeFn, args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if f == 0u32 || usize(f) > natives.len()
        return fault(at, "this builtin was never registered, which is a defect in slate itself")

    natives.at(usize(f) - 1).go(args, at)
end dispatch_native

// -- what this file registers ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **Four groups, and each one is here because its implementations are.** `private` in sysl is
// private to the FILE, so the `do_*` below can only be registered from inside it -- which is the
// whole shape of the rule: a subsystem's natives, the block that registers them and the scope they
// are declared into stay together, and no central list has to learn a new name.

// The handful that belong to no module: what a program prints with, what every value answers to, and
// the four object operations.
var n_print: NativeFn = 0

// **Characters, not UTF-16 units.** Reached only as `.length` on a string, an array and a range --
// there is no global `len`, the property being the one spelling.
var n_length: NativeFn = 0

var n_push: NativeFn = 0

// **The type words double as the conversions**, so `string` tests in a pattern and converts in an
// expression.
var n_string: NativeFn = 0

// **The four universal methods**, reached on any value that has not put a field of the same name in
// front of them. `method.sysl` says why they are the only names allowed to do that.
var n_to_string: NativeFn = 0
var n_eq: NativeFn = 0
var n_ne: NativeFn = 0
var n_equals: NativeFn = 0

var n_keys: NativeFn = 0
var n_has: NativeFn = 0

// **`without` and not `remove`**, which `slate:fs` took for deleting a file. An object is not
// changed, so the participle is right anyway: `sorted` answers a new array and this answers a new
// object.
var n_without: NativeFn = 0

install_basics(env: *EnvObj)
    n_print = register("print", do_print)
    n_length = register("length", do_len)
    n_push = register("push", do_push)
    n_string = register("string", do_string)
    n_to_string = register("toString", do_to_string)
    n_eq = register("eq", (args, at) -> do_eq(args, at, true))
    n_ne = register("ne", (args, at) -> do_eq(args, at, false))
    n_equals = register("equals", do_equals)
    n_keys = register("keys", do_keys)
    n_has = register("has", do_has)
    n_without = register("without", do_without)

    declare(env, "print", Native(n_print), false)
    declare(env, "push", Native(n_push), false)
    declare(env, "string", Native(n_string), false)
    declare(env, "keys", Native(n_keys), false)

    // **The other two ways to walk an object.** `entries` is what makes a destructuring `for` head
    // worth having -- `for [k, v] in entries(o)` is the loop every program over a record wants.
    declare(env, "has", Native(n_has), false)

    // **The one object operation there was no way to write.** `keys`, `values`, `entries` and `has`
    // read, and `with` writes -- so a table that had to forget something had to be rebuilt by hand
    // out of everything else, which is what `slate-language/sluice`'s session store does.
    declare(env, "without", Native(n_without), false)
end install_basics

// The event loop's, which are node's names because a program that knows node should not have to
// learn new ones for the same thing.
var n_set_timeout: NativeFn = 0
var n_set_interval: NativeFn = 0
var n_clear_timeout: NativeFn = 0

// Promises. `sleep` is the one that makes `await` worth having on its own -- everything else a
// program can build out of an async function.
var n_sleep: NativeFn = 0
var n_resolve: NativeFn = 0
var n_reject: NativeFn = 0

// **A promise nobody has answered yet, and the two ways of answering it.** `resolve` and `reject`
// make a promise that has already settled, which is the easy half; these are the half that bridges a
// callback into `await`, and without them a program can only wait for something slate itself
// started. TC39's `Promise.withResolvers` is the same three pieces.
var n_pending: NativeFn = 0
var n_settle: NativeFn = 0
var n_fail: NativeFn = 0

install_timers(env: *EnvObj)
    n_set_timeout = register("setTimeout", (args, at) -> do_timer(args, at, false))
    n_set_interval = register("setInterval", (args, at) -> do_timer(args, at, true))
    n_clear_timeout = register("clearTimeout", do_clear)
    n_sleep = register("sleep", do_sleep)
    n_resolve = register("resolve", (args, at) -> do_settled(args, at, false))
    n_reject = register("reject", (args, at) -> do_settled(args, at, true))
    n_pending = register("pending", do_pending)
    n_settle = register("settle", (args, at) -> do_answer(args, at, false))
    n_fail = register("fail", (args, at) -> do_answer(args, at, true))

    declare(env, "setTimeout", Native(n_set_timeout), false)
    declare(env, "setInterval", Native(n_set_interval), false)
    declare(env, "clearTimeout", Native(n_clear_timeout), false)

    // node has both names for one operation, and so does slate.
    declare(env, "clearInterval", Native(n_clear_timeout), false)

    // **Bare names where JavaScript has `Promise.resolve` and `Promise.reject`.** slate has no
    // namespaces yet, and inventing one for three functions would be inventing it for the wrong
    // reason.
    declare(env, "sleep", Native(n_sleep), false)
    declare(env, "resolve", Native(n_resolve), false)
    declare(env, "reject", Native(n_reject), false)
    declare(env, "pending", Native(n_pending), false)
    declare(env, "settle", Native(n_settle), false)
    declare(env, "fail", Native(n_fail), false)
end install_timers

// TCP. **A socket is a handle with a lifetime**, which is what makes this different from the file
// system: `connect` and `send` answer promises, and `listen` and `onData` take callbacks, because a
// stream is a run of events rather than one answer.
var n_listen: NativeFn = 0
var n_connect: NativeFn = 0
var n_on_data: NativeFn = 0
var n_on_bytes: NativeFn = 0
var n_on_error: NativeFn = 0
var n_send: NativeFn = 0
var n_close_socket: NativeFn = 0
var n_local_port: NativeFn = 0

// **Who is at the other end**, which a server has no other way to learn -- `slate:http` told a
// handler the method, the path and the headers and nothing at all about the client.
var n_remote_address: NativeFn = 0
var n_start_tls: NativeFn = 0

// **Which application protocol a TLS connection settled on**, which is the one question a server
// taking both HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 on one port cannot answer any other way.
var n_alpn_protocol: NativeFn = 0

// **TCP, which is `slate:net` and no longer global.**
//
// **`send`, `close` and `connect` are why this module went third.** They are words a program wants
// for its own -- a mailer sends, a file closes -- and while they were global a script writing
// `close(handle) = ...` shadowed a builtin without meaning to.
install_net(env: *EnvObj, everything: bool)
    n_listen = register("listen", do_listen)
    n_connect = register("connect", do_connect)
    n_on_data = register("onData", (args, at) -> do_read(args, at, true))
    n_on_bytes = register("onBytes", (args, at) -> do_read(args, at, false))
    n_on_error = register("onError", do_on_error)
    n_send = register("send", do_send)
    n_close_socket = register("close", do_close)
    n_local_port = register("localPort", do_local_port)
    n_remote_address = register("remoteAddress", do_remote_address)
    n_start_tls = register("startTls", do_start_tls)
    n_alpn_protocol = register("alpnProtocol", do_alpn_protocol)

    if everything
        declare(env, "listen", Native(n_listen), false)
        declare(env, "connect", Native(n_connect), false)
        declare(env, "onData", Native(n_on_data), false)
        declare(env, "onBytes", Native(n_on_bytes), false)
        declare(env, "onError", Native(n_on_error), false)
        declare(env, "send", Native(n_send), false)
        declare(env, "close", Native(n_close_socket), false)
        declare(env, "localPort", Native(n_local_port), false)
        declare(env, "remoteAddress", Native(n_remote_address), false)
        declare(env, "startTls", Native(n_start_tls), false)
        declare(env, "alpnProtocol", Native(n_alpn_protocol), false)
end install_net

// The file system. **node's names**, and the promise-shaped thirteen come first.
var n_read_file: NativeFn = 0
var n_read_bytes: NativeFn = 0
var n_write_file: NativeFn = 0
var n_append_file: NativeFn = 0

// **Bytes on the writing side, which is the reading `send` already had.** `writeFile` renders
// anything that is not a string the way `print` does, so an array reached the disk as its own text --
// and a program holding an image or a compressed body had to encode it to store it.
var n_write_bytes: NativeFn = 0
var n_append_bytes: NativeFn = 0

// **Where a program says what went wrong.** `print` is the answer a program produces and this is
// what it says about producing it, which is the division every shell already makes: `slate app.sl >
// answers.txt` keeps the answers and lets the complaints through. In `slate:process` rather than
// `slate:fs`, being about this program rather than about a file.
var n_stderr: NativeFn = 0

var n_read_dir: NativeFn = 0
var n_stat: NativeFn = 0
var n_exists: NativeFn = 0
var n_remove: NativeFn = 0
var n_mkdir: NativeFn = 0
var n_rmdir: NativeFn = 0
var n_rename: NativeFn = 0

// The same thirteen, blocking. **`Sync` is node's suffix and the reason for having them is node's
// too**: a program that reads its configuration before it starts, or a script whose whole job is
// three file operations in order, gains nothing from a promise and pays for it in ceremony.
var n_read_file_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_read_bytes_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_write_file_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_append_file_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_write_bytes_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_append_bytes_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_read_dir_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_stat_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_exists_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_remove_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_mkdir_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_rmdir_sync: NativeFn = 0
var n_rename_sync: NativeFn = 0

// **The file system, which is `slate:fs` and no longer global.**
//
// **`stat`, `exists`, `remove` and `rename` are why this module went second.** They are verbs a
// program wants for its own, and while they were global a script writing `val stat = ...` shadowed a
// builtin without meaning to.
//
// **The blocking ten go through the same argument checking as the others**, which is what `with_path`
// taking a function pointer bought: a `Sync` builtin differs from its asynchronous twin only in which
// function it hands over.
install_fs(env: *EnvObj, everything: bool)
    n_read_file = register("readFile", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "readFile", read_file_promise))
    n_read_bytes = register("readBytes", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "readBytes", read_bytes_promise))
    n_read_dir = register("readDir", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "readDir", read_dir_promise))
    n_stat = register("stat", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "stat", stat_promise))
    n_exists = register("exists", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "exists", exists_promise))
    n_remove = register("remove", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "remove", remove_promise))
    n_mkdir = register("mkdir", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "mkdir", mkdir_promise))
    n_rmdir = register("rmdir", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "rmdir", rmdir_promise))
    n_write_file = register("writeFile", do_write_file)
    n_stderr = register("stderr", do_stderr)
    n_append_file = register("appendFile", do_append_file)
    n_write_bytes = register("writeBytes", (args, at) -> with_bytes(args, at, "writeBytes", write_bytes_promise))
    n_append_bytes = register("appendBytes", (args, at) -> with_bytes(args, at, "appendBytes", append_bytes_promise))
    n_rename = register("rename", do_rename)

    n_read_file_sync = register("readFileSync", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "readFileSync", read_file_sync))
    n_read_bytes_sync = register("readBytesSync", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "readBytesSync", read_bytes_sync))
    n_read_dir_sync = register("readDirSync", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "readDirSync", read_dir_sync))
    n_stat_sync = register("statSync", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "statSync", stat_sync))
    n_exists_sync = register("existsSync", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "existsSync", exists_sync))
    n_remove_sync = register("removeSync", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "removeSync", remove_sync))
    n_mkdir_sync = register("mkdirSync", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "mkdirSync", mkdir_sync))
    n_rmdir_sync = register("rmdirSync", (args, at) -> with_path(args, at, "rmdirSync", rmdir_sync))
    n_write_file_sync = register("writeFileSync", do_write_file_sync)
    n_append_file_sync = register("appendFileSync", do_append_file_sync)
    n_write_bytes_sync = register("writeBytesSync", (args, at) -> with_bytes(args, at, "writeBytesSync", write_bytes_sync))
    n_append_bytes_sync = register("appendBytesSync", (args, at) -> with_bytes(args, at, "appendBytesSync", append_bytes_sync))
    n_rename_sync = register("renameSync", do_rename_sync)

    if everything
        declare(env, "readFile", Native(n_read_file), false)
        declare(env, "readBytes", Native(n_read_bytes), false)
        declare(env, "writeFile", Native(n_write_file), false)
        declare(env, "appendFile", Native(n_append_file), false)
        declare(env, "writeBytes", Native(n_write_bytes), false)
        declare(env, "appendBytes", Native(n_append_bytes), false)
        declare(env, "readDir", Native(n_read_dir), false)
        declare(env, "stat", Native(n_stat), false)
        declare(env, "exists", Native(n_exists), false)
        declare(env, "remove", Native(n_remove), false)
        declare(env, "mkdir", Native(n_mkdir), false)
        declare(env, "rmdir", Native(n_rmdir), false)
        declare(env, "rename", Native(n_rename), false)

        declare(env, "readFileSync", Native(n_read_file_sync), false)
        declare(env, "readBytesSync", Native(n_read_bytes_sync), false)
        declare(env, "writeFileSync", Native(n_write_file_sync), false)
        declare(env, "appendFileSync", Native(n_append_file_sync), false)
        declare(env, "writeBytesSync", Native(n_write_bytes_sync), false)
        declare(env, "appendBytesSync", Native(n_append_bytes_sync), false)
        declare(env, "readDirSync", Native(n_read_dir_sync), false)
        declare(env, "statSync", Native(n_stat_sync), false)
        declare(env, "existsSync", Native(n_exists_sync), false)
        declare(env, "removeSync", Native(n_remove_sync), false)
        declare(env, "mkdirSync", Native(n_mkdir_sync), false)
        declare(env, "rmdirSync", Native(n_rmdir_sync), false)
        declare(env, "renameSync", Native(n_rename_sync), false)

        // Declared with `slate:process`'s names rather than `slate:fs`'s: what this program says
        // about its own run belongs beside `exit` and `args`.
        declare(env, "stderr", Native(n_stderr), false)
end install_fs


// Whether these arguments begin with an array, which is what tells the array form of a shared name
// from the text one. A wrong first argument falls through to the text form and is complained about
// there, where the message already names what it wanted.
// Not `private`: `text.sysl` registers `contains`, `indexOf` and `lastIndexOf`, the three names that
// answer about an array or about a string, and this is what tells the two apart.
first_is_array(args: Buf[Value]) -> bool
    if args.is_empty() then return false

    args.at(0) match
        Array(_) -> true
        _ -> false
end first_is_array

// Whether these arguments begin with a buffer of bytes, which is the third reading the three shared
// names have: `indexOf` finds a byte or a run of bytes in one, where the array form compares values
// and the string form compares characters.
first_is_bytes(args: Buf[Value]) -> bool
    if args.is_empty() then return false

    args.at(0) match
        Bytes(_) -> true
        _ -> false
end first_is_bytes


// -- TCP ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A port, which a program writes as a number and nothing else.
private port_of(v: Value, at: Span, name: string) -> Result[long, Signal]
    v match
        Int(n) -> Ok(n)
        other -> Err(failure(at, s"`${name}` takes a port number, and this is ${other.type_name()}"))

private do_listen(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`listen` takes a port and a function, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val (port, secure, reuse_port) = listen_target(args.at(0), at)?

    listen_socket(port, secure, reuse_port, args.at(1), at)

private do_connect(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`connect` takes an address and a port, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    args.at(0) match
        Str(p) -> connect_socket(p.s, port_of(args.at(1), at, "connect")?, at)
        other -> fault(at, s"`connect` takes an address, and this is ${other.type_name()}")

private do_read(args: Buf[Value], at: Span, as_text: bool) -> Step
    val name = if as_text then "onData" else "onBytes"

    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`${name}` takes a socket and a function, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    read_socket(args.at(0), args.at(1), as_text, at)

private do_on_error(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`onError` takes a socket and a function, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    error_socket(args.at(0), args.at(1), at)

private do_send(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`send` takes a socket and what to send, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    args.at(1) match
        Str(p) -> send_socket(args.at(0), p.s, at)

        // **An array is BYTES and not its own rendering.** `send(sock, [72, 105])` meaning the six
        // characters `[72, 105]` is not something anybody wants, and bytes are how a program sends
        // what is not text -- an image, a compressed body, a string it read with `onBytes` and never
        // decoded.
        // **A buffer is the shape a program reaches for now**, and it is the cheap one: the bytes are
        // already bytes, so nothing is decoded on the way to the socket.
        Bytes(p) -> send_bytes(args.at(0), p.data.view(), at)
        Array(p) ->
            val payload = bytes_of(p, at, "send")?

            send_bytes(args.at(0), payload.view(), at)

        // Rendered as `print` would render it, which is the reading `writeFile` already takes.
        other -> send_socket(args.at(0), printed(other, at)?, at)

private do_close(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`close` takes a socket and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    close_socket(args.at(0), at)

// `alpnProtocol(conn)` -- the name the two ends agreed on, or `null` where there is none.
private do_alpn_protocol(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`alpnProtocol` takes a connection and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    alpn_protocol(args.at(0), at)
end do_alpn_protocol

private do_local_port(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`localPort` takes a socket and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    local_port(args.at(0), at)

// `remoteAddress(conn)` -- the IP at the other end, or `null` where there is no other end.
private do_remote_address(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`remoteAddress` takes a connection and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    remote_address(args.at(0), at)

private do_start_tls(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`startTls` takes a socket and the host to connect as, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    start_tls_target(args.at(1), at)? match
        AsClient(host, trust, alpn) -> start_tls_socket(args.at(0), host, trust.view(), alpn.view(), at)
        AsServer(certificate, key, alpn) -> present_tls_socket(args.at(0), certificate.view(), key.view(), alpn.view(), at)

// The path a file-system builtin was given, or the complaint about what it was given instead.
//
// Not `private`: nothing outside needs it, but it is the shape every one of them shares and pulling
// it out is what keeps ten builtins from being ten copies of the same three lines.
private path_of(v: Value, at: Span, name: string) -> Result[string, Signal]
    v match
        Str(p) -> Ok(p.s)
        other -> Err(failure(at, s"`${name}` takes a path, and this is ${other.type_name()}"))

// The eight that take one path and nothing else.
//
// **A function pointer rather than eight arms**, which is what the shape is worth: each of them
// differs only in which libuv call it makes, and writing the argument check eight times is how the
// eighth one ends up saying something the other seven do not.
private with_path(args: Buf[Value], at: Span, name: string, go: &Fn(string, Span) -> Step) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`${name}` takes one path and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    go(path_of(args.at(0), at, name)?, at)
end with_path

// The four that take a path and an array of bytes.
//
// **Anything that is not an array of bytes FAULTS here, where `writeFile` renders it**, and the two
// readings are the same rule seen from opposite ends: `writeFile` is about a file's text, so a
// number is something a reader would want written out, while `writeBytes` is about the bytes
// themselves and a value that is not bytes is a mistake in the program. Rendering it would put
// `[72, 105]` on the disk under a name that says otherwise.
private with_bytes(args: Buf[Value], at: Span, name: string, go: &Fn(string, Buf[u8], Span) -> Step) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`${name}` takes a path and its bytes, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val path = path_of(args.at(0), at, name)?

    args.at(1) match
        Bytes(p) -> go(path, p.data, at)
        Array(p) -> go(path, bytes_of(p, at, name)?, at)
        other -> fault(at, s"`${name}` takes bytes or an array of numbers, and this is ${other.type_name()} -- `${write_twin(name)}` is what writes anything else")
end with_bytes

// The name a program reaching `writeBytes` with text was probably after.
private write_twin(name: string) -> string =
    name match
        "writeBytes" -> "writeFile"
        "appendBytes" -> "appendFile"
        "writeBytesSync" -> "writeFileSync"
        else "appendFileSync"

private do_write_file(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`writeFile` takes a path and its contents, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val path = path_of(args.at(0), at, "writeFile")?

    args.at(1) match
        Str(p) -> write_file_promise(path, p.s, at)

        // **Anything else is rendered as `print` would render it**, which is the reading a program
        // writing a number or an array to a file expects -- and the alternative, refusing everything
        // but a string, would make `writeFile(path, str(x))` the only spelling anybody ever wrote.
        other -> write_file_promise(path, printed(other, at)?, at)
end do_write_file

// `stderr(text)` -- one line on the diagnostic stream.
//
// **Straight to the descriptor rather than over libuv, and it answers nothing.** A complaint is the
// one thing a program may need to have said before the next line runs -- a crash after an `await`
// would lose it -- and every other way of writing here is asynchronous. `print` is synchronous for
// the same reason and this is `print`'s sibling.
//
// **The newline is the caller's**, as node's `process.stderr.write` leaves it: a program writing a
// JSON object per line and one writing a progress bar want different answers, and only one of them
// can be the default.
private do_stderr(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    var out = str_builder()

    for i in 0..<args.len()
        if i > 0 then out.push_char(' ')

        out.push(printed(args.at(i), at)?)

    eprints(out.finish())
    Ok(Null)
end do_stderr

// `appendFile(path, text)` -- `writeFile` that adds rather than replaces.
private do_append_file(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`appendFile` takes a path and what to add, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val path = path_of(args.at(0), at, "appendFile")?

    args.at(1) match
        Str(p) -> append_file_promise(path, p.s, at)
        other -> append_file_promise(path, printed(other, at)?, at)
end do_append_file

private do_write_file_sync(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`writeFileSync` takes a path and its contents, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val path = path_of(args.at(0), at, "writeFileSync")?

    args.at(1) match
        Str(p) -> write_file_sync(path, p.s, at)
        other -> write_file_sync(path, printed(other, at)?, at)

// `appendFileSync(path, text)` -- `appendFile` for a program that is not on the loop.
//
// **Every operation `slate:fs` has answers under a `Sync` suffix, and this one did not.** The page
// says every one has a blocking twin, ten of them had, and the eleventh arrived with the promise
// half alone -- so a sink, which answers nothing and cannot await, had no way to write a line and
// know it had landed.
private do_append_file_sync(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`appendFileSync` takes a path and what to add, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val path = path_of(args.at(0), at, "appendFileSync")?

    args.at(1) match
        Str(p) -> append_file_sync(path, p.s, at)
        other -> append_file_sync(path, printed(other, at)?, at)

private do_rename_sync(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`renameSync` takes two paths and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    rename_sync(path_of(args.at(0), at, "renameSync")?, path_of(args.at(1), at, "renameSync")?, at)

private do_rename(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`rename` takes two paths and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    rename_promise(path_of(args.at(0), at, "rename")?, path_of(args.at(1), at, "rename")?, at)

// `sleep(ms)` -- a promise for later.
private do_sleep(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`sleep` takes a number of milliseconds and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    args.at(0) match
        Int(n) -> sleep_promise(n, at)
        Real(r) -> sleep_promise(long(r), at)

        // **A duration says what a bare number cannot**, and is why `sleep(2.seconds())` reads
        // better than `sleep(2000)`. The number form stays, being what node writes and what every
        // program here already says.
        DurationVal(us) -> sleep_promise(us / 1000, at)
        other -> fault(at, s"`sleep` takes a number of milliseconds or a duration, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_sleep

// `resolve(v)` and `reject(message)` -- a promise that has already settled.
//
// **Worth having even though an `async` function makes both**, because the two are what a program
// reaches for when it is adapting something that is not async at all: a cache hit that needs to look
// like a lookup, a validation that fails before any work starts.
private do_settled(args: Buf[Value], at: Span, failing: bool) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1
        val what = if failing then "`reject`" else "`resolve`"

        return fault(at, s"${what} takes one argument and was given ${args.len()}")

    val mark = root_mark()
    val pr = new_promise()

    hold(Promise(pr))

    if failing then settle_err(pr, printed(args.at(0), at)?, at, current().current_source) else settle_ok(pr, args.at(0))

    release(mark)
    Ok(Promise(pr))
end do_settled

// `pending()` -- a promise nobody has answered yet -- and `settle(p, v)` and `fail(p, message)`,
// which answer it.
//
// **This is the bridge from a callback into `await`.** `resolve` and `reject` adapt something that
// has already happened; these adapt something that has not, which is the case a program cannot write
// its way out of: without them the only things awaitable are the ones slate itself started, and
// anything with a callback of its own -- a library that calls back when a download finishes, a queue
// handing out a job, the end of a request body -- stays in callbacks and cannot be awaited at all.
//
// **A promise settles once, and a second answer is dropped rather than refused.** That is
// `settle_ok`'s rule rather than a new one, and it is JavaScript's: two callbacks racing to answer
// one promise is a program deciding late, not a program in error.
private do_pending(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 0
        return fault(at, s"`pending` takes no arguments and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    Ok(Promise(new_promise()))
end do_pending

private do_answer(args: Buf[Value], at: Span, failing: bool) -> Step
    val name = if failing then "fail" else "settle"

    if args.len() != 2
        return fault(at, s"`${name}` takes a promise and an answer, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val pr = args.at(0) match
        Promise(p) -> p
        other -> return fault(at, s"`${name}` answers a promise, and this is ${other.type_name()}")

    if failing then settle_err(pr, printed(args.at(1), at)?, at, current().current_source) else settle_ok(pr, args.at(1))

    Ok(Null)
end do_answer

// `setTimeout(fn, ms)`; the delay may be left out, and means the next turn of the loop.
private do_timer(args: Buf[Value], at: Span, repeating: bool) -> Step
    if args.len() == 0 || args.len() > 2
        return fault(at, s"a timer takes a function and a delay, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val ms = if args.len() == 2
        args.at(1) match
            Int(n) -> n
            Real(r) -> long(r)
            DurationVal(us) -> us / 1000
            other -> return fault(at, s"a delay is a number of milliseconds or a duration, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
    else
        0

    set_timer(args.at(0), ms, repeating, at)
end do_timer

private do_clear(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"cancelling a timer takes its id, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    clear_timer(args.at(0), at)

// `print(a, b, ...)` -- every argument rendered, a space between, one line.
//
// **Reaches `live_sink` at once when `live_output` is set, flushed behind it.** `putbytes` goes
// through C's `putchar`, and C block-buffers a stream that is not a terminal -- so a program piped
// to a file or a supervisor's journal would say nothing until it exited, which a program that never
// exits says never. `flush()` is one syscall per line, the same price node pays on every
// `console.log`.
private do_print(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    var out = str_builder()

    for i in 0..<args.len()
        if i > 0 then out.push_char(' ')

        out.push(printed(args.at(i), at)?)

    val line = out.finish()

    val v = current()

    v.output.push(line)

    if v.live_output
        v.live_sink(line)
        flush()

    Ok(Null)
end do_print

// `.length` -- of an array, a string or a range. An object counts its keys through `keys(o).length`
// instead, `.length` being a property no name on an object may be given.
private do_len(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`.length` takes one argument and was given ${args.len()}")

    args.at(0) match
        Array(a) -> Ok(Int(long(a.items.len())))

        // **Bytes, because that is what a buffer holds.** This is the number a `Content-Length` and
        // every length prefix on a wire is, which is why `toBytes(s).length` has always been how a
        // slate program asks for it.
        Bytes(b) -> Ok(Int(long(b.data.len())))
        // **Characters, not bytes.** `"日本語".length` is three; it answered nine until the day the
        // rule was settled, and every slice taken from that count cut a character in half.
        Str(p) -> Ok(Int(long(char_count(p.s))))

        // How many whole numbers it covers, which is exactly how many turns a `for` over it takes.
        RangeVal(lo, hi, inclusive, has_lo, has_hi, step) ->
            if !has_lo || !has_hi
                return fault(at, "a range with an end left out has no length until it is used on something")

            Ok(Int(long(range_count(lo, hi, inclusive, step))))
        other -> fault(at, s"`.length` needs an array, bytes, a string or a range, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_len

// `push(xs, v)` -- onto the end of an array, which it changes.
//
// **It answers nothing, as every mutator here does.** Handing the array back is what JavaScript does
// and is what makes `xs.push(1).push(2)` read like a pipeline over fresh values when it is two
// writes to one array; `array.sysl` carries the rule and the rest of the family.
private do_push(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`push` takes two arguments and was given ${args.len()}")

    args.at(0) match
        Array(a) ->
            keepable(args.at(1), at, "stored")?
            array_push(a, args.at(1))
            Ok(Null)

        // **ONE BYTE OR A WHOLE BUFFER, and the second is the reason this kind exists.** Appending
        // one buffer to another used to be a loop in the program, one `Value` per byte; here it is
        // an `extend`, which is a copy of a run of memory. A number is a byte and must be one --
        // `push(b, 256)` is a mistake in the program, not a truncation.
        Bytes(b) -> args.at(1) match
            Int(_) ->
                bytes_push(b, byte_of_value(args.at(1), at)?)
                Ok(Null)
            Bytes(other) ->
                // **`push(b, b)` IS COPIED FIRST, and that is not fussiness**: `extend` reads a view
                // into the very storage it is growing, which a reallocation moves out from under it.
                val ba: *u8 = ptr_cast(b)
                val oa: *u8 = ptr_cast(other)

                if ba == oa
                    var copy: Buf[u8] = buf()

                    copy.extend(other.data.view())
                    bytes_extend(b, copy.view())
                else
                    bytes_extend(b, other.data.view())

                Ok(Null)
            other -> fault(at, s"`push` onto bytes takes a byte or more bytes, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
        other -> fault(at, s"`push` needs an array or bytes, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_push

// `str(x)` -- what `print` would have shown.
private do_string(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`string` takes one argument and was given ${args.len()}")

    Ok(str_value(printed(args.at(0), at)?))

// `v.toString()` -- what `print` would show, and what a class may say for itself.
//
// **Reached only where the value has no `toString` of its own**, the universal table being the last
// thing a method lookup tries -- so this never recurses into the hook that would have replaced it.
private do_to_string(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`toString` takes no arguments and was given ${args.len() - 1}")

    Ok(str_value(printed(args.at(0), at)?))

// `a.eq(b)` and `a.ne(b)` -- the same OBJECT, not the same shape.
private do_eq(args: Buf[Value], at: Span, want: bool) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`${if want then "eq" else "ne"}` takes one argument and was given ${args.len() - 1}")

    Ok(Bool(identical(args.at(0), args.at(1), at) == want))

// `a.equals(b)` -- `==` under its own name, hooks and all.
//
// **The two may not disagree**, which is why this is `same` rather than a second reading of it: a
// class that writes an `equals` changes both at once, and a class that writes none gets identity from
// both because that is what `same` now does for a nominal value.
private do_equals(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`equals` takes one argument and was given ${args.len() - 1}")

    Ok(Bool(same(args.at(0), args.at(1), at)?))

// `keys(o)` -- an object's field names, as an array of strings.
private do_keys(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`keys` takes one argument and was given ${args.len()}")

    args.at(0) match
        Object(o) ->
            // In the order the fields were first written, which the table preserves.
            Ok(Array(new_array(obj_keys(o))))
        other -> fault(at, s"`keys` needs an object, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_keys

// Whether an object has a key at all.
//
// **This is the only way to tell an absent field from one holding `null`.** slate has one absent value
// where JavaScript has two, so reading answers `null` either way -- and the recovery is to ask the
// question rather than to read and inspect. Python draws the line in the same place with `in`, and Go
// with the second result of a map read.
private do_has(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`has` takes an object and a key, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    args.at(0) match
        // **A key the walks hide is a key the object does not have**, so `has(p, "proto")` on a class
        // instance is false where `keys(p)` no longer lists it. Two answers to one question is what
        // the four walks had between them before this.
        Object(o) ->
            if !walked_key(o, args.at(1)) then return Ok(Bool(false))

            Ok(Bool(obj_has(o, args.at(1), at)?))
        other -> fault(at, s"`has` needs an object, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_has

// `without(o, key)` -- the object again, with that key gone.
//
// **A NEW object, because a slate object's shape is not a thing a program changes in place.** `with`
// is the other half of this and answers a copy for the same reason; a `delete` writing through the
// value would make every name holding it see the field go, which is the surprise `with` was built to
// avoid.
//
// **A key that is not there is not an error**, so `without` is idempotent and a program removing a
// key it is unsure of writes one line. **A data value's copy is a data value**, exactly as `with`'s
// is -- and a hidden key cannot be removed, there being nothing there to a program that asks.
private do_without(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`without` takes an object and a key, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    args.at(0) match
        Object(fields) ->
            val key = args.at(1)
            val mark = root_mark()
            val o = new_object()

            hold(Object(o))

            for i in 0..<fields.entries.len()
                val e = fields.entries.at(i)

                if !e.dead && !(walked_key(fields, e.key) && same(e.key, key, at)?)
                    obj_put(o, e.key, e.value, at)?

            // Set last, the writes above being how the copy was filled -- `WithObject`'s own
            // ordering and for its reason.
            o.frozen = fields.frozen

            release(mark)
            Ok(Object(o))
        other -> fault(at, s"`without` needs an object, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_without


// The sentence a program gets for reaching the JavaScript host through an external where there is
// none.
//
// **It says which host it is running in, which is `no_document`'s rule** -- the program is correct
// and the command is the mistake, the same source being right when it is compiled with `slate js`.
// **The declaration itself never reaches this**, so a module that binds a host value can be imported
// by a program that never asks one anything: what faults is the operation that would have to ask.
js_host_only(what: string) -> string =
    s"${what} reaches the JavaScript host, and the interpreter has no JavaScript host -- an external names a value of the host that a program compiled with `slate js` runs in"

